WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
The most common words that languages
borrow from each other

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.600
are nouns, words for things, probably
since it's fairly straightforward

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.880
to point at an object,
say the word for it,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.760
and have the other person understand
that’s what you’re talking about.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.780
Things like "fire" or "chicken" are
easier concepts to communicate

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.360
than, say, "awesome" or "the",

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.480
and probably more immediately useful
for trade and the basics of living.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.440
Also, there are so many things that
humans give different names to.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.080
So if you go through a list of loanwords
into a particular language,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.900
lots of them will be
creatures and objects

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.040
that speakers of that language would
never have encountered at home.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.300
That’s the first of the two main ways
that words get moved between languages:

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.620
as loanwords.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.320
Through most of history,
people would go to a new place,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.260
ask what the name of a local thing is,
get a response,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.360
and then start calling the thing that name.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.160
Sometimes, though, the word they were given
wasn't a name so much as a

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.440
description of what the thing was,
or a category.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.460
We don't know the exact derivation,
but some North American languages

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.760
use a word like "pakan" for any hard nut.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.960
But it became the English word for
the "PE-can" nut. Or "pe-CAN" nut.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.860
The Arabic word /sˤaħ.raːʔ/ for "deserts"
became the English word

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.460
specifically for the Sahara Desert.
So the Desert Desert.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.520
Which keeps happening!
Matcha tea is "powdered tea tea".

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.840
The La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles
are "the ‘the tar’ tar pits".

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.820
Timor Leste is East the East.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.040
There’s even the Milky Way Galaxy,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.220
galaxy comes from the Ancient Greek
"gala" meaning milk,

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.220
presumably because it looks like
milk spilled across the sky?

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.720
There’s a lot of these!

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.060
And that’s if the
translation stays intact!

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.680
There’s a Tupi word, mbokaʔẽ́,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.440
which describes a way of
cooking meat over a fire,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.780
that was borrowed into
French as "boucan."

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.020
Someone who used that method of
cooking meat because they were,

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.500
say, on a long voyage,
was therefore a "boucanier,"

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.660
or in English a "buccaneer".

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.900
Or the word for a king in Persian,
/ʃɑːh/, was eventually

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.160
borrowed over and over again

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.220
across multiple languages
over centuries of trading

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.340
to arrive in English as the words
"chess" and "check".

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.080
And "shah," of course.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.080
Sometimes we borrow a word
that we already have,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.580
it’s just travelled by
a different route

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.200
and been changed in
two different ways!

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.880
Those are called doublets.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.080
"Grain" and "corn" both come from
the same Proto-Indo-European root,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.780
one directly inherited through
English's Germanic roots,

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.400
and one through Romance languages.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.120
"Ward" and "guard" both come
from French at different times.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.440
And "anime" comes from Japan, which
borrowed "animation" from English!

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.000
Often, when a word’s being borrowed,
the grammar doesn’t quite work out,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.580
the new language keeps the
other bits that are clinging on,

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.440
called affixes and clitics,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.260
and those get baked into
the loanword itself.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.160
Loads of words in English that start
with al- come from Arabic.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.220
Albatross comes from al-ġaṭṭās,
"the diver".

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.000
Algebra from al-jabr, which originally
meant healing and resetting broken bones

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.580
The term for this is "rebracketing".

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.080
My favourite is admiral,

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.020
which is said to come from amīr-al-mā
"commander of the water"

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.840
and amīr-al-bahr
"commander of the sea".

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.680
So "admiral" is sort of
"commander of the",

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.080
and then we just lost what
they were commander of.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.380
But English received those
loanwords from French,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.540
which might have got them from Latin?

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.800
So every time they moved language
they got… well, remodelled a bit

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.840
as speakers adapted them
to sound local.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.520
Because of course we do!

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:25.320
Iif I suddenly go all Spanish mid-sentence
for something like "Puerrrrto Rrrrrico",

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.780
I either sound pretentious or foolish

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.600
or claiming to be a lot better
at Spanish than I actually am.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.580
I’m also going to
use that as an excuse

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.000
for all my appalling pronunciations
of non-English words in this video.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.560
I’m sorry to the rest of the world.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.020
..there’s a British sentence.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.760
So those are loanwords.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.780
The second way that words get translated
across languages is much simpler,

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.880
and those words are called "calques".

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.400
This is where a word is divided
into its component parts,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.380
each of which then gets translated.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.140
The most well-known example
is "skyscraper".

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.820
In the first half of the
twentieth century,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.160
many languages were suddenly
in need of a word

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.800
to describe buildings that are
really, really big.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.820
Now, tall sails on a ship were
already called ‘sky scrapers’,

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.120
two words, and it’s possible that’s where
the word for the buildings came from,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.520
it got hyphenated and
then combined over time,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.960
like a lot of words do.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.280
But even languages who never had
the concept of ‘sky scraper’ sails

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.620
just translated the two parts.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.260
And that leads to my favourite bit
of this whole thing:

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.220
loanword is a copy of the German
word for the concept, Lehnwort.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.580
It has its two parts separately
translated and joined back together.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.080
Calque, on the other hand, is
borrowed directly from French.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.060
It means "to copy or trace".

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.660
Which means that "loanword"
is a calque,

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.060
and "calque" is a loanword.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.360
If you want to know more about this,
then my co-author Gretchen McCulloch

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.360
has an entire episode of the podcast
"Lingthusiasm" about it,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.800
the link is in the description.

